From: builder <builder@winlator>
Subject: [PATCH] fexcore: treat RBP as non-TSO like RSP

FEX skips the TSO barrier for RSP-relative accesses because the stack is
thread-private. Apply the same rule to RBP, which addresses a frame on that
stack in frame-pointer code.

Heuristic: RBP is a general-purpose register, so a pointer to shared memory
kept in RBP loses TSO ordering.

---
diff --git a/FEXCore/Source/Interface/Core/OpcodeDispatcher.h b/FEXCore/Source/Interface/Core/OpcodeDispatcher.h
index 4477c72..75f03e9 100644
--- a/FEXCore/Source/Interface/Core/OpcodeDispatcher.h
+++ b/FEXCore/Source/Interface/Core/OpcodeDispatcher.h
@@ -1599,9 +1599,14 @@ private:
     return !(Load && (Operand.IsLiteral() || Operand.IsLiteralRelocation() || Operand.IsLiteralPatchable())) && !Operand.IsGPR();
   }
 
+  // RSP-relative accesses are thread-private stack memory, so the TSO barrier
+  // buys nothing. RBP addresses a frame on the same stack in frame-pointer
+  // code; treating it the same is a heuristic, since RBP can also hold a
+  // pointer to shared memory.
   [[nodiscard]]
   static bool IsNonTSOReg(MemoryAccessType Access, uint8_t Reg) {
-    return Access == MemoryAccessType::DEFAULT && Reg == X86State::REG_RSP;
+    return Access == MemoryAccessType::DEFAULT &&
+           (Reg == X86State::REG_RSP || Reg == X86State::REG_RBP);
   }
 
   AddressMode DecodeAddress(const X86Tables::DecodedOp& Op, const X86Tables::DecodedOperand& Operand, MemoryAccessType AccessType, bool IsLoad);
